{"responses_create_params":{"background":null,"include":null,"input":[{"content":"You are opencode, an interactive CLI tool that helps users with software engineering tasks. Use the instructions below and the tools available to you to assist the user.\n\nIMPORTANT: You must NEVER generate or guess URLs for the user unless you are confident that the URLs are for helping the user with programming. You may use URLs provided by the user in their messages or local files.\n\nIf the user asks for help or wants to give feedback inform them of the following:\n- /help: Get help with using opencode\n- To give feedback, users should report the issue at https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues\n\nWhen the user directly asks about opencode (eg 'can opencode do...', 'does opencode have...') or asks in second person (eg 'are you able...', 'can you do...'), first use the WebFetch tool to gather information to answer the question from opencode docs at https://opencode.ai\n\n# Tone and style\nYou should be concise, direct, and to the point. When you run a non-trivial bash command, you should explain what the command does and why you are running it, to make sure the user understands what you are doing (this is especially important when you are running a command that will make changes to the user's system).\nRemember that your output will be displayed on a command line interface. Your responses can use GitHub-flavored markdown for formatting, and will be rendered in a monospace font using the CommonMark specification.\nOutput text to communicate with the user; all text you output outside of tool use is displayed to the user. Only use tools to complete tasks. Never use tools like Bash or code comments as means to communicate with the user during the session.\nIf you cannot or will not help the user with something, please do not say why or what it could lead to, since this comes across as preachy and annoying. Please offer helpful alternatives if possible, and otherwise keep your response to 1-2 sentences.\nOnly use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid using emojis in all communication unless asked.\nIMPORTANT: You should minimize output tokens as much as possible while maintaining helpfulness, quality, and accuracy. Only address the specific query or task at hand, avoiding tangential information unless absolutely critical for completing the request. If you can answer in 1-3 sentences or a short paragraph, please do.\nIMPORTANT: You should NOT answer with unnecessary preamble or postamble (such as explaining your code or summarizing your action), unless the user asks you to.\nIMPORTANT: Keep your responses short, since they will be displayed on a command line interface. You MUST answer concisely with fewer than 4 lines (not including tool use or code generation), unless user asks for detail. Answer the user's question directly, without elaboration, explanation, or details. One word answers are best. Avoid introductions, conclusions, and explanations. You MUST avoid text before/after your response, such as \"The answer is <answer>.\", \"Here is the content of the file...\" or \"Based on the information provided, the answer is...\" or \"Here is what I will do next...\". Here are some examples to demonstrate appropriate verbosity:\n<example>\nuser: what is 2+2?\nassistant: 4\n</example>\n\n<example>\nuser: is 11 a prime number?\nassistant: Yes\n</example>\n\n<example>\nuser: what command should I run to list files in the current directory?\nassistant: ls\n</example>\n\n<example>\nuser: what command should I run to watch files in the current directory?\nassistant: [use the ls tool to list the files in the current directory, then read docs/commands in the relevant file to find out how to watch files]\nnpm run dev\n</example>\n\n<example>\nuser: what files are in the directory src/?\nassistant: [runs ls and sees foo.c, bar.c, baz.c]\nuser: which file contains the implementation of foo?\nassistant: src/foo.c\n</example>\n\n<example>\nuser: write tests for new feature\nassistant: [uses grep and glob search tools to find where similar tests are defined, uses concurrent read file tool use blocks in one tool call to read relevant files at the same time, uses edit file tool to write new tests]\n</example>\n\n# Proactiveness\nYou are allowed to be proactive, but only when the user asks you to do something. You should strive to strike a balance between:\n1. Doing the right thing when asked, including taking actions and follow-up actions\n2. Not surprising the user with actions you take without asking\nFor example, if the user asks you how to approach something, you should do your best to answer their question first, and not immediately jump into taking actions.\n3. Do not add additional code explanation summary unless requested by the user. After working on a file, just stop, rather than providing an explanation of what you did.\n\n# Following conventions\nWhen making changes to files, first understand the file's code conventions. Mimic code style, use existing libraries and utilities, and follow existing patterns.\n- NEVER assume that a given library is available, even if it is well known. Whenever you write code that uses a library or framework, first check that this codebase already uses the given library. For example, you might look at neighboring files, or check the package.json (or cargo.toml, and so on depending on the language).\n- When you create a new component, first look at existing components to see how they're written; then consider framework choice, naming conventions, typing, and other conventions.\n- When you edit a piece of code, first look at the code's surrounding context (especially its imports) to understand the code's choice of frameworks and libraries. Then consider how to make the given change in a way that is most idiomatic.\n- Always follow security best practices. Never introduce code that exposes or logs secrets and keys. Never commit secrets or keys to the repository.\n\n# Code style\n- IMPORTANT: DO NOT ADD ***ANY*** COMMENTS unless asked\n\n# Doing tasks\nThe user will primarily request you perform software engineering tasks. This includes solving bugs, adding new functionality, refactoring code, explaining code, and more. For these tasks the following steps are recommended:\n- Use the available search tools to understand the codebase and the user's query. You are encouraged to use the search tools extensively both in parallel and sequentially.\n- Implement the solution using all tools available to you\n- Verify the solution if possible with tests. NEVER assume specific test framework or test script. Check the README or search codebase to determine the testing approach.\n- VERY IMPORTANT: When you have completed a task, you MUST run the lint and typecheck commands (e.g. npm run lint, npm run typecheck, ruff, etc.) with Bash if they were provided to you to ensure your code is correct. If you are unable to find the correct command, ask the user for the command to run and if they supply it, proactively suggest writing it to AGENTS.md so that you will know to run it next time.\nNEVER commit changes unless the user explicitly asks you to. It is VERY IMPORTANT to only commit when explicitly asked, otherwise the user will feel that you are being too proactive.\n\n- Tool results and user messages may include <system-reminder> tags. <system-reminder> tags contain useful information and reminders. They are NOT part of the user's provided input or the tool result.\n\n# Tool usage policy\n- When doing file search, prefer to use the Task tool in order to reduce context usage.\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. When making multiple bash tool calls, you MUST send a single message with multiple tools calls to run the calls in parallel. For example, if you need to run \"git status\" and \"git diff\", send a single message with two tool calls to run the calls in parallel.\n\nYou MUST answer concisely with fewer than 4 lines of text (not including tool use or code generation), unless user asks for detail.\n\nIMPORTANT: Before you begin work, think about what the code you're editing is supposed to do based on the filenames directory structure.\n\n# Code References\n\nWhen referencing specific functions or pieces of code include the pattern `file_path:line_number` to allow the user to easily navigate to the source code location.\n\n<example>\nuser: Where are errors from the client handled?\nassistant: Clients are marked as failed in the `connectToServer` function in src/services/process.ts:712.\n</example>\n\nYou are powered by the model named dummy_model. The exact model ID is nemo_gym/dummy_model\nHere is some useful information about the environment you are running in:\n<env>\n  Working directory: /testbed\n  Workspace root folder: /testbed\n  Is directory a git repo: yes\n  Platform: linux\n  Today's date: Tue Aug 04 2026\n</env>\nSkills provide specialized instructions and workflows for specific tasks.\nUse the skill tool to load a skill when a task matches its description.\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>customize-opencode</name>\n    <description>Use ONLY when the user is editing or creating opencode's own configuration: opencode.json, opencode.jsonc, files under .opencode/, or files under ~/.config/opencode/. Also use when creating or fixing opencode agents, subagents, skills, plugins, MCP servers, or permission rules. Do not use for the user's own application code, or for any project that is not configuring opencode itself.</description>\n    <location>file:///testbed/%3Cbuilt-in%3E</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>","role":"system","type":"message","phase":null},{"content":"Read /workspace/task/TASK_INSTRUCTIONS.txt and the referenced files in /workspace/task. Complete the task and save only final deliverables in /workspace/output. Do not access paths outside /workspace. If required information is absent, search the web.","role":"user","type":"message","phase":null}],"instructions":null,"max_output_tokens":null,"max_tool_calls":null,"metadata":null,"model":null,"parallel_tool_calls":true,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt":null,"reasoning":null,"service_tier":null,"store":null,"context_management":null,"conversation":null,"moderation":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":null,"safety_identifier":null,"stream_options":null,"temperature":null,"text":null,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[],"top_logprobs":null,"top_p":null,"truncation":null,"user":null,"stream":null},"response":{"id":"resp_03ac0be8d6f1470a94dac40c7b4cb15d","created_at":1788466310.0,"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"instructions":null,"metadata":null,"model":"policy_model","object":"response","output":[{"id":"rs_36f5495fd68e41ce8ae48c672477997a","summary":[{"text":"Let me start by reading the task instructions.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"arguments":"{\"filePath\": \"/workspace/task/TASK_INSTRUCTIONS.txt\"}","call_id":"read_0_8b710a1a","name":"read","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"read_0_8b710a1a","output":"<path>/workspace/task/TASK_INSTRUCTIONS.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are preparing the Statistical Analysis Plan for Phase III trial XR-2847 evaluating cardiovascular disease prevention. The sponsor requires validation of statistical assumptions against empirical data and regulatory alignment before protocol finalization.\n2: \n3: Using the Framingham Heart Study cohort as your reference population, verify that study eligibility criteria (per the clinical study proposal) yield a population whose observed event rate supports the planned sample size assumptions. Operationalize the observable proposal exclusions by excluding records with prevalentStroke=1 (the available proxy for prior CVD history) or diabetes=1; the proposal's upper sysBP bound already excludes severe hypertension. Because the dataset has no chronic kidney disease field and no broader prior-CVD indicator beyond prevalentStroke, document those limitations explicitly rather than treating them as observed exclusions. Document findings in \"Eligibility_Analysis.xlsx\" with population flow tracking subject counts at each filtering step.\n4: \n5: Develop \"Sample_Size_Justification.xlsx\" containing the primary calculation using protocol-specified parameters, a recalculation using the empirically-derived control rate, and a sensitivity matrix spanning power levels and clinically meaningful effect sizes. The analysis must account for the planned group sequential design with two interim looks. Your written recommendation should address what regulatory guidance indicates about relying on empirical versus assumed event rates for sample size determination.\n6: \n7: Perform stratified baseline characterization in \"Baseline_Subgroup_Analysis.xlsx\" across pre-specified subgroups examining continuous risk factors within each stratum. Identify strata where event rates suggest potential effect modification and note any strata with insufficient sample sizes for meaningful analysis. Extend this workbook with missing data assessment: determine which variable has highest missingness, evaluate whether this missingness is associated with outcomes, and recommend an appropriate handling strategy.\n8: \n9: Consult the FDA's ICH E9 guidance, \"E9 Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials,\" for regulatory citations, and use Wan Nor Arifin's Sample Size Calculator v2.0 spreadsheet, obtained separately, to cross-validate your sample size computations.\n10: \n11: Local Reference Files:\n12: - Clinical_Study_Proposal_CVD_Prevention.csv\n13: - framingham.csv\n\n(End of file - total 13 lines)\n</content>","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"filePath\": \"/workspace/task\"}","call_id":"read_1_7525fbfe","name":"read","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"read_1_7525fbfe","output":"<path>/workspace/task</path>\n<type>directory</type>\n<entries>\nClinical_Study_Proposal_CVD_Prevention.csv\nframingham.csv\nTASK_INSTRUCTIONS.txt\n\n(3 entries)\n</entries>","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"id":"rs_6c70c6a36c64460fa699b5b1fa0c8006","summary":[{"text":"Let me read the reference files.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"arguments":"{\"filePath\": \"/workspace/task/Clinical_Study_Proposal_CVD_Prevention.csv\"}","call_id":"read_0_1fbdf405","name":"read","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"read_0_1fbdf405","output":"<path>/workspace/task/Clinical_Study_Proposal_CVD_Prevention.csv</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: Study_Parameter,Value,Notes\n2: Study_Title,Phase III Randomized Controlled Trial of Novel Antihypertensive Agent XR-2847 for Cardiovascular Disease Prevention,\n3: Primary_Objective,Evaluate efficacy of XR-2847 vs placebo in reducing 10-year cardiovascular disease risk,\n4: Study_Design,Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial,1:1 randomization\n5: Study_Duration_Years,5,With 3-year follow-up extension option\n6: Primary_Endpoint,Composite cardiovascular events (MI stroke or CV death),Binary outcome\n7: Primary_Endpoint_Type,Binary,Event vs No Event\n8: Expected_Event_Rate_Control,0.15,15% based on Framingham risk predictions for target population\n9: Expected_Event_Rate_Treatment,0.10,33% relative risk reduction target\n10: Minimum_Detectable_Difference,0.05,Absolute risk reduction\n11: Significance_Level_Alpha,0.05,Two-sided\n12: Statistical_Power,0.80,80% power\n13: Statistical_Power_Sensitivity,0.90,90% power for sensitivity analysis\n14: Dropout_Rate_Anticipated,0.15,15% anticipated dropout over study duration\n15: Allocation_Ratio,1:1,Equal allocation to treatment and control\n16: Target_Population,Adults aged 45-70 with moderate cardiovascular risk,Framingham Risk Score 10-20%\n17: Inclusion_Criteria_Age_Min,45,Years\n18: Inclusion_Criteria_Age_Max,70,Years\n19: Inclusion_Criteria_SysBP_Min,130,mmHg - Stage 1 hypertension or higher\n20: Inclusion_Criteria_SysBP_Max,159,mmHg - Not severe hypertension\n21: Exclusion_Criteria,History of CVD event or diabetes or severe hypertension or chronic kidney disease,\n22: Number_of_Sites,25,Multi-center study\n23: Countries,USA Canada UK Germany France,5 countries\n24: Interim_Analyses_Planned,2,At 50% and 75% information\n25: Interim_Analysis_Method,O'Brien-Fleming spending function,Alpha spending approach\n26: Safety_Monitoring,Independent Data Safety Monitoring Board,Quarterly reviews\n27: Subgroup_Analyses_Planned,Age gender baseline risk score smoking status,Pre-specified subgroups\n28: Secondary_Endpoints,All-cause mortality and hospitalization for heart failure and blood pressure reduction,\n29: Budget_Per_Patient_USD,8500,Estimated cost per enrolled patient\n30: Regulatory_Requirements,FDA IND approval and IRB approval at all sites,\n31: Statistical_Analysis_Plan,ITT primary analysis with per-protocol sensitivity,\n32: Missing_Data_Approach,Multiple imputation under MAR assumption,\n\n(End of file - total 32 lines)\n</content>","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"head -3 /workspace/task/framingham.csv && wc -l /workspace/task/framingham.csv\", \"description\": \"Preview framingham dataset structure\"}","call_id":"bash_1_b1371b11","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_1_b1371b11","output":"male,age,education,currentSmoker,cigsPerDay,BPMeds,prevalentStroke,prevalentHyp,diabetes,totChol,sysBP,diaBP,BMI,heartRate,glucose,TenYearCHD\n1,39,4,0,0,0,0,0,0,195,106,70,26.97,80,77,0\n0,46,2,0,0,0,0,0,0,250,121,81,28.73,95,76,0\n4240 /workspace/task/framingham.csv\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"id":"rs_537ce1b0f29c47b39e59bca491fcbbd6","summary":[{"text":"Plan:\n1. Load framingham.csv, apply eligibility filters stepwise: age 45-70, sysBP 130-159, exclude prevalentStroke=1, exclude diabetes=1. Track population flow. Compute observed event rate (TenYearCHD).\n2. Sample size: two-proportion comparison, p1=0.15, p2=0.10, alpha=0.05 two-sided, power 0.80, 1:1. Adjust for group sequential design with 2 interim looks (O'Brien-Fleming) — use inflation factor ~1.022 for OBF with 3 looks total (2 interims + final). Recalculate with empirical control rate. Sensitivity matrix over power (0.80, 0.90) and effect sizes (e.g., RRR 25%, 30%, 33%, 35%, 40% or absolute differences). Adjust for 15% dropout.\n3. Baseline subgroup analysis: strata by age group, gender, smoking status, baseline risk — continuous risk factors (sysBP, totChol, BMI, glucose, etc.) within strata, event rates per stratum, flag small strata. Missing data: variable with highest missingness, association with outcome (compare missing vs not missing event rates), recommendation.\n4. Write 3 xlsx files to /workspace/output. Also note ICH E9 citations — maybe include in a written recommendation within Sample_Size_Justification.xlsx.\n\nLet me check Python availability and pandas/openpyxl.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"python3 -c \\\"import pandas, numpy, openpyxl, scipy; print('ok')\\\"\", \"description\": \"Check Python libraries availability\"}","call_id":"bash_0_26dcefcc","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_0_26dcefcc","output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'scipy'\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"ls /workspace/output 2>/dev/null || mkdir -p /workspace/output && ls /workspace\", \"description\": \"Ensure output directory exists\"}","call_id":"bash_1_96961340","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_1_96961340","output":"Workspace-Bench\noutput\ntask\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"pip install scipy -q 2>&1 | tail -1; python3 -c \\\"import scipy; print(scipy.__version__)\\\"\", \"description\": \"Install scipy for statistical calculations\"}","call_id":"bash_0_d6d12024","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_0_d6d12024","output":"hint: See PEP 668 for the detailed specification.\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'scipy'\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\nimport pandas as pd\\ndf = pd.read_csv('/workspace/task/framingham.csv')\\nprint(df.shape)\\nprint(df.dtypes)\\nprint(df.isna().sum())\\nEOF\", \"description\": \"Inspect framingham data types and missingness\"}","call_id":"bash_1_f3123757","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_1_f3123757","output":"(4240, 16)\nmale                 int64\nage                  int64\neducation          float64\ncurrentSmoker        int64\ncigsPerDay         float64\nBPMeds             float64\nprevalentStroke      int64\nprevalentHyp         int64\ndiabetes             int64\ntotChol            float64\nsysBP              float64\ndiaBP              float64\nBMI                float64\nheartRate          float64\nglucose            float64\nTenYearCHD           int64\ndtype: object\nmale                 0\nage                  0\neducation          105\ncurrentSmoker        0\ncigsPerDay          29\nBPMeds              53\nprevalentStroke      0\nprevalentHyp         0\ndiabetes             0\ntotChol             50\nsysBP                0\ndiaBP                0\nBMI                 19\nheartRate            1\nglucose            388\nTenYearCHD           0\ndtype: int64\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"pip install --break-system-packages scipy -q 2>&1 | tail -1; python3 -c \\\"from scipy.stats import norm; print('ok')\\\"\", \"description\": \"Install scipy with system override\"}","call_id":"bash_0_ccd30dfb","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_0_ccd30dfb","output":"WARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager. It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv\nok\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"id":"rs_4b6ae0208c5b4b48b85f1881c8975e32","summary":[{"text":"Now let me plan the analysis:\n\n1. Eligibility filtering:\n   - Start: 4240\n   - Age 45-70\n   - sysBP 130-159\n   - Exclude prevalentStroke=1, diabetes=1\n   - Document CKD limitation (no field) and prior CVD limitation (only prevalentStroke proxy)\n\n2. Observed event rate in eligible population (TenYearCHD).\n\n3. Sample size:\n   - Primary: p1=0.15, p2=0.10, alpha=0.05 two-sided, power=0.80, 1:1\n   - Recalc with empirical control rate, keep 33% RRR? Or keep absolute 0.05? Task says \"recalculation using the empirically-derived control rate\" — use empirical control rate with the same 33% relative risk reduction (treatment = control*(1-0.33)), and maybe also same absolute difference. I'll use same RRR.\n   - Sensitivity matrix: power 0.80/0.90 × effect sizes (RRR 25%, 30%, 33%, 40% or absolute differences).\n   - Group sequential design: 2 interim looks at 50%, 75% info, O'Brien-Fleming → inflation factor ~1.008-1.011. OBF inflation factor for 3 analyses (2 interim + final): approximately 1.017? Standard values: for 3 looks at 33/67/100%, OBF IF ≈ 1.017. For looks at 50/75/100%, IF ≈ 1.008. Let me just use 1.008 and document.\n   - Dropout inflation 15%.\n\n4. Baseline subgroup analysis: subgroups age (45-54, 55-64, 65-70), gender, smoking status, baseline risk (can't compute FRS easily; use sysBP or prevalentHyp? Proposal subgroups: age, gender, baseline risk score, smoking status). Baseline risk score — could approximate with glucose/totChol... Simpler: use predicted risk via simple stratification by prevalentHyp or by number of risk factors. I'll define baseline risk strata via a simple count of risk factors (smoking, high chol≥240, BMI≥30) or use median split of a simple risk proxy. Actually I could compute a simple logistic-based predicted probability from Framingham itself... Simpler: stratify by totChol or use tertiles of a crude risk score. I'll compute a quick logistic regression of TenYearCHD on age, male, sysBP, totChol, currentSmoker, glucose, BMI using statsmodels... may not be installed. Could just use sklearn? Simplest: use numpy least squares logistic via IRLS hand-coded, or just stratify by observed risk factor count. I'll do \"baseline risk\" strata: Low (<3 risk factors), High (≥3) from {male, currentSmoker, totChol>=240, BMI>=30, glucose>=126}. Hmm, better: tertiles of 10-year predicted risk using simple score = sum of points. Keep it simple and documented.\n\n   Within each stratum: continuous risk factors means (age, sysBP, diaBP, totChol, BMI, glucose), event rates, n. Identify effect modification (differences in event rates) and strata with insufficient n (say <30 or <50).\n\n5. Missing data: glucose 388 highest. Evaluate association with outcome: compare TenYearCHD rate in glucose-missing vs observed; also compare in eligible population. Recommend handling (MAR assumption, multiple imputation per proposal).\n\n6. Fetch ICH E9 citation quotes. Task says consult FDA's ICH E9 guidance. Let me webfetch a bit for relevant passages about event rate assumptions. And Wan Nor Arifin's calculator — cross-validate: his two-proportion formula uses standard formula. I can mention cross-validation against the spreadsheet's formula n per group = ... I'll do the standard formula and note agreement.\n\nLet me fetch ICH E9 briefly.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"id":"msg_57b360f0e21542f299fcb6d27be37a62","content":[{"annotations":[],"text":"Now let me get the ICH E9 reference for regulatory citations.","type":"output_text","logprobs":null}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":null},{"arguments":"{\"url\": \"https://www.fda.gov/media/71336/download\", \"format\": \"text\"}","call_id":"webfetch_0_29127724","name":"webfetch","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"webfetch_0_29127724","output":"%PDF-1.2 \r\n%����\r\n \r\n8 0 obj\r\n<<\r\n/Length 9 0 R\r\n/Filter /FlateDecode \r\n>>\r\nstream\r\nH�}��n�0\u0010E��\u007f�e���\r,��6�UM���\u0005'qE 2��\u007f��\u000eQ�J\u0015�56��w\u001e�m�JP�H\u0004���\b���Ct�H@dx��b�\u0010N8�%\\�O�{\u001al�����u�n�����\f�\u001f\u0011�X�����\u0011��\u0004�D�\u000bH�\u0010A�\u001c6��m��R���lS�Sm:O^Զ\u0019�=��$a\u0010s\u0019\f\u0003\"Y\u0016���uVם�\u001bS��D̕�:�<\tI�\u0015��\u0013Iex��l\u0012(�I��h�\u001e�\u001d����\u0003覂�p�\rl�;��\u0004\u0011�F?T&A\u0003Y,����\u000b��m+O(ܰ���)a�0�\t^\u0015��P��d�y�HY�#�|Q<gy���\u0019��b:�]y���E�|y�MU��0�jb+!��維۽-��\u0004�W\u0001�5\u00157\u0002����89i~QؘSo��(B�<\u000bs�n�Ɔ���z��ז8�\fR>�Nd9ާ�����d�;%p+R����7����\r\nendstream\r\nendobj\r\n9 0 obj\r\n394\r\nendobj\r\n4 0 obj\r\n<<\r\n/Type /Page\r\n/Parent 5 0 R\r\n/Resources <<\r\n/Font <<\r\n/F0 6 0 R \r\n>>\r\n/ProcSet 2 0 R\r\n>>\r\n/Contents 8 0 R\r\n>>\r\nendobj\r\n13 0 obj\r\n<<\r\n/Length 14 0 R\r\n/Filter /FlateDecode \r\n>>\r\nstream\r\nH���ێ�0\u0010���w��\"�`;�i{�r(+u��B�^p�M\f��\u0003r\u0002�o��pضR+nF�|���g�ò\u0017S\u0014��g\b-'=��GmPo8È%���a\u001f\u00078�:C]yDw\u001f�2�U&кV���M�~���{/B^�j Ҙ\u0000�0\u00043�0̖���h��V6��x����2�+Dc��BV�{�%)�)�C+\u0017\u0001�&\u0016h