uld add note. Acceptable.\n2. Cholesterol tertile: low tertile has HIGHER event rate (23.9%) than high (18.3%) — counterintuitive, likely due to missingness/age confounding. My effect-modification text says \"Event rate increases across cholesterol tertiles\" — WRONG. Need fix.\n3. The proposal's subgroups were age, gender, baseline risk score, smoking status. I used cholesterol tertile as \"baseline risk\" proxy — better to build an actual risk score? Could compute Framingham-like risk or simply note proxy. I'll update interpretation text for cholesterol tertile to reflect observed non-monotonic pattern.\n\nFix the effect modification text for cholesterol tertile. Also, the flags for HIGH/LOW rates: overall 0.1958; thresholds HIGH = rate > 0.1958*1.5=0.2937, LOW < 0.1175. 65-70 would be HIGH but N<100 excluded. Fine.\n\nAlso the recalc with fixed 33% RRR gives smaller N (1195) — consistent.\n\nUpdate cholesterol text.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"arguments":"{\"filePath\": \"/workspace/output/build_analysis.py\", \"oldString\": \" ('Cholesterol tertile', 'Event rate increases across cholesterol tertiles.'),\\n ('Insufficient strata', 'Any stratum flagged INSUFFICIENT N (<100 subjects) yields unstable rate estimates (e.g., 45-54 band upper tail or thin cells) and should not drive design decisions.'),\", \"newString\": \" ('Cholesterol tertile', 'Used as the observable proxy for the pre-specified \\\"baseline risk score\\\" subgroup. Event rates are NON-MONOTONIC across tertiles (low tertile highest), likely reflecting confounding (age/sex mix across cholesterol levels) and totChol missingness; do not interpret as evidence of modification - an interaction test on a proper multivariable baseline risk score is recommended in the trial.'),\\n ('Insufficient strata', 'The 65-70 age stratum (N=68) is flagged INSUFFICIENT N: despite having the highest observed event rate (~35%), its estimate is unstable (wide CI) and should not drive design or subgroup claims. Strata with N<100 should not support effect-modification inferences.'),\"}","call_id":"edit_0_82560c6c","name":"edit","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"edit_0_82560c6c","output":"Edit applied successfully.","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"python3 /workspace/output/build_analysis.py && rm /workspace/output/build_analysis.py && ls -la /workspace/output\", \"description\": \"Final rebuild, remove script, list deliverables\"}","call_id":"bash_0_fef5161e","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_0_fef5161e","output":"eligible N=1108 events=217 rate=0.1958\nprimary n/group=682.9\nglucose missing chi2 p=0.2108\ndone\ntotal 32\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root  132 Sep  3 20:11 .\ndrwxr-xr-x. 1 root root   44 Sep  3 20:06 ..\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root 9378 Sep  3 20:11 Baseline_Subgroup_Analysis.xlsx\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root 7211 Sep  3 20:11 Eligibility_Analysis.xlsx\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root 8626 Sep  3 20:11 Sample_Size_Justification.xlsx\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"id":"msg_0cba38e43041426abad1f13bb025d950","content":[{"annotations":[],"text":"Done. Three deliverables in `/workspace/output`:\n\n**Eligibility_Analysis.xlsx** — Population flow: 4240 → 2813 (age 45-70) → 1166 (sysBP 130-159) → 1152 (exclude prevalentStroke) → 1108 (exclude diabetes). Observed 10-yr CHD rate = 19.6% (217/1108) vs assumed 15%. Limitations sheet documents that CKD has no field and prior CVD is only proxied by prevalentStroke (partial operationalization).\n\n**Sample_Size_Justification.xlsx** — Primary calc (15% vs 10%, 80% power): 683/group → 1366 → 1389 with O'Brien-Fleming 3-look inflation (1.017) → 1635 to enroll with 15% dropout; 90%-power sensitivity: 2188. Empirical-rate recalcs (19.6% control, 5% ARR: 2120; 33% RRR: 1195) plus a 3×4 power × effect-size sensitivity matrix. Recommendation cites ICH E9 §3.5 (state assumptions, examine sensitivity) and §4.3 (pre-specified OBF spending), cross-validates against Wan Nor Arifin's calculator (identical formula), and advises retaining protocol assumptions with a blinded event-rate re-estimation at interim, since the empirical rate is an upper bound given unmodelled exclusions.\n\n**Baseline_Subgroup_Analysis.xlsx** — Strata by age, sex, smoking, sysBP band, cholesterol tertile with means of sysBP/chol/BMI/glucose/age; event-rate gradients flag potential effect modification (age, sex, smoking, BP); 65-70 stratum (N=68) flagged insufficient. Missing data: glucose highest at 9.2%; chi-square vs TenYearCHD p=0.21 (no outcome association, consistent with MCAR/MAR); recommends multiple imputation under MAR per ICH E9 §5.3 with delta-adjustment sensitivity.","type":"output_text","logprobs":null}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":null}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"temperature":null,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[],"top_p":null,"background":null,"completed_at":null,"conversation":null,"max_output_tokens":null,"max_tool_calls":null,"moderation":null,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":null,"reasoning":null,"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":null,"status":null,"text":null,"top_logprobs":null,"truncation":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":96511,"input_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":594944},"output_tokens":9321,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3264},"total_tokens":704040},"user":null},"reward":0.3889,"failure_reason":null,"opencode_results_fpath":"/Users/cmunley/Documents/Codex/2026-09-02/g/work/Gym/responses_api_agents/opencode_sandboxed_agent/results/86d21b8a-708d-4a96-ad43-3547e6eb99fa/export.json","opencode_run_stdout":"Shell: /bin/bash\nInstalled OpenCode\n<think>Let me start by reading the task instructions.</think>\n<think>Let me read the reference files.</think>\n<think>Plan:\n1. Load framingham.csv, apply eligibility filters stepwise: age 45-70, sysBP 130-159, exclude prevalentStroke=1, exclude diabetes=1. Track population flow. Compute observed event rate (TenYearCHD).\n2. Sample size: two-proportion comparison, p1=0.15, p2=0.10, alpha=0.05 two-sided, power 0.80, 1:1. Adjust for group sequential design with 2 interim looks (O'Brien-Fleming) — use inflation factor ~1.022 for OBF with 3 looks total (2 interims + final). Recalculate with empirical control rate. Sensitivity matrix over power (0.80, 0.90) and effect sizes (e.g., RRR 25%, 30%, 33%, 35%, 40% or absolute differences). Adjust for 15% dropout.\n3. Baseline subgroup analysis: strata by age group, gender, smoking status, baseline risk — continuous risk factors (sysBP, totChol, BMI, glucose, etc.) within strata, event rates per stratum, flag small strata. Missing data: variable with highest missingness, association with outcome (compare missing vs not missing event rates), recommendation.\n4. Write 3 xlsx files to /workspace/output. Also note ICH E9 citations — maybe include in a written recommendation within Sample_Size_Justification.xlsx.\n\n\nLet me check Python availability and pandas/openpyxl.</think>\n<think>Now let me plan the analysis:\n\n\n1. Eligibility filtering:\n   - Start: 4240\n   - Age 45-70\n   - sysBP 130-159\n   - Exclude prevalentStroke=1, diabetes=1\n   - Document CKD limitation (no field) and prior CVD limitation (only prevalentStroke proxy)\n\n\n2. Observed event rate in eligible population (TenYearCHD).\n\n\n3. Sample size:\n   - Primary: p1=0.15, p2=0.10, alpha=0.05 two-sided, power=0.80, 1:1\n   - Recalc with empirical control rate, keep 33% RRR? Or keep absolute 0.05? Task says \"recalculation using the empirically-derived control rate\" — use empirical control rate with the same 33% relative risk reduction (treatment = control*(1-0.33)), and maybe also same absolute difference. I'll use same RRR.\n   - Sensitivity matrix: power 0.80/0.90 × effect sizes (RRR 25%, 30%, 33%, 40% or absolute differences).\n   - Group sequential design: 2 interim looks at 50%, 75% info, O'Brien-Fleming → inflation factor ~1.008-1.011. OBF inflation factor for 3 analyses (2 interim + final): approximately 1.017? Standard values: for 3 looks at 33/67/100%, OBF IF ≈ 1.017. For looks at 50/75/100%, IF ≈ 1.008. Let me just use 1.008 and document.\n   - Dropout inflation 15%.\n\n\n4. Baseline subgroup analysis: subgroups age (45-54, 55-64, 65-70), gender, smoking status, baseline risk (can't compute FRS easily; use sysBP or prevalentHyp? Proposal subgroups: age, gender, baseline risk score, smoking status). Baseline risk score — could approximate with glucose/totChol... Simpler: use predicted risk via simple stratification by prevalentHyp or by number of risk factors. I'll define baseline risk strata via a simple count of risk factors (smoking, high chol≥240, BMI≥30) or use median split of a simple risk proxy. Actually I could compute a simple logistic-based predicted probability from Framingham itself... Simpler: stratify by totChol or use tertiles of a crude risk score. I'll compute a quick logistic regression of TenYearCHD on age, male, sysBP, totChol, currentSmoker, glucose, BMI using statsmodels... may not be installed. Could just use sklearn? Simplest: use numpy least squares logistic via IRLS hand-coded, or just stratify by observed risk factor count. I'll do \"baseline risk\" strata: Low (<3 risk factors), High (≥3) from {male, currentSmoker, totChol>=240, BMI>=30, glucose>=126}. Hmm, better: tertiles of 10-year predicted risk using simple score = sum of points. Keep it simple and documented.\n\n\n   Within each stratum: continuous risk factors means (age, sysBP, diaBP, totChol, BMI, glucose), event rates, n. Identify effect modification (differences in event rates) and strata with insufficient n (say <30 or <50).\n\n\n5. Missing data: glucose 388 highest. Evaluate association with outcome: compare TenYearCHD rate in glucose-missing vs observed; also compare