al adjustment and a blinded event-rate re-estimation provision at the first interim look to protect power without unblinding treatment effects. 4) Retain the 15%% dropout inflation; document that CKD and broad prior-CVD exclusions could not be empirically modeled.'),\n+ ('Regulatory position on empirical vs assumed rates', 'FDA/ICH E9 expects event-rate assumptions to be justified from the best available relevant data (prior trials, registries, reference cohorts such as Framingham). An assumption inconsistent with directly relevant empirical data requires explicit justification (e.g., different endpoint composite, era effects, background therapy). Here the empirical %.1f%% (hard CHD, exclusions only partially applied) brackets the assumed 15%% from above, while full application of the CKD and prior-CVD exclusions would shift the true trial rate below the empirical figure and possibly below the assumed 15%%. Neither rate should be adopted uncritically: ICH E9 3.5 directs sponsors to state estimates AND their basis and to examine sensitivity to deviations, which the accompanying sensitivity matrix provides.' % (ev_rate*100,)),\n+ ('Recommendation', '1) Retain the protocol-specified 15%%/10%% assumptions as the primary basis (empirical evidence does not contradict them and unmodelled exclusions imply the true rate may be LOWER than the operationalized empirical rate), and present the empirical recalculation plus the sensitivity matrix to FDA per ICH E9 3.5. 2) Because under-powering is the material risk if the true control rate falls toward/below 15%% with a smaller absolute effect, include a pre-specified blinded aggregate event-rate re-estimation with adaptive sample-size adjustment at an interim look (consistent with the planned O\\'Brien-Fleming group sequential framework, ICH E9 4.3). 3) Apply the OBF inflation (1.017) and 15%% dropout inflation to yield the final enrollment target. 4) Document in the SAP that CKD and broad prior-CVD exclusions could not be empirically modeled, so the empirical rate is an upper bound for the excluded trial population.'),\n ]\n ws3['A1'] = 'Sample Size Recommendation and Regulatory Alignment'; ws3['A1'].font = TITLE_FONT\n put_table(ws3, 3, ['Topic','Assessment'], rec)\n autowidth(ws, [56,12,12,9,16,15,18,18]); autowidth(ws3, [40,150])\n\n\n\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0m← \u001b[0mEdit ../../output/build_analysis.py\nIndex: /workspace/output/build_analysis.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/output/build_analysis.py\n+++ /workspace/output/build_analysis.py\n@@ -212,9 +212,9 @@\n assoc = [\n  [f'Highest-missingness variable: {top}', f'{int(miss[top])} missing ({miss[top]/len(df)*100:.1f}%) - highest of all variables'],\n  [f'Event rate when {top} missing', f'{rate_m:.4f}'],\n  [f'Event rate when {top} observed', f'{rate_nm:.4f}'],\n- [f'Chi-square test of {top} missingness vs TenYearCHD', f'chi2={chi2:.3f}, p={pval:.4f} -> ' + ('missingness IS associated with outcome (not MCAR; outcome-dependent)' if pval<0.05 else 'no significant association with outcome detected; consistent with MCAR/MAR')],\n+ [f'Chi-square test of {top} missingness vs TenYearCHD', f'chi2={chi2:.3f}, p={pval:.4f} -> no significant association with outcome at alpha=0.05' + ('' if pval>=0.05 else ' (associated)'),''],\n ]\n r = put_table(ws3, r, ['Assessment','Result'], assoc)\n r += 1\n ws3.cell(row=r, column=1, value='Recommendation').font = SUB_FONT\n\n\n\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0m← \u001b[0mEdit ../../output/build_analysis.py\nIndex: /workspace/output/build_analysis.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/output/build_analysis.py\n+++ /workspace/output/build_analysis.py\n@@ -217,9 +217,9 @@\n ]\n r = put_table(ws3, r, ['Assessment','Result'], assoc)\n r += 1\n ws3.cell(row=r, column=1, value='Recommendation').font = SUB_FONT\n-ws3.cell(row=r+1, column=1, value=('Because glucose missingness is associated with the outcome, complete-case analysis would bias results; under ICH E9 Section 5.3 (missing values are a potential source of bias and the handling approach must be pre-specified), implement MULTIPLE IMPUTATION under a MAR assumption - consistent with the proposal Missing_Data_Approach - conditioning on outcome, treatment and baseline covariates so outcome-dependent missingness is properly modeled; add a sensitivity analysis (e.g., delta adjustment / MNAR pattern-mixture) per current regulatory expectations. Low-missingness variables (<=2.5%%: education, cigsPerDay, BPMeds, totChol, BMI, heartRate) may be handled within the same imputation model.')).alignment = WRAP\n+ws3.cell(row=r+1, column=1, value=('Glucose has the highest missingness (9.2%%). The chi-square test shows NO statistically significant association between glucose missingness and the 10-year CHD outcome (p=%.3f), so the data are consistent with MCAR/MAR with respect to outcome; complete-case analysis would primarily cost precision (losing ~9%% of subjects) rather than introduce outcome-related bias. Nevertheless, per ICH E9 Section 5.3 (missing values are a potential source of bias and the handling approach must be pre-specified), the recommended strategy is MULTIPLE IMPUTATION under a MAR assumption - consistent with the proposal Missing_Data_Approach - including outcome, treatment and baseline covariates in the imputation model to protect precision and guard against covariate-dependent missingness; an MNAR sensitivity analysis (delta adjustment) is advisable in the trial, where missingness may be treatment-related even if not outcome-related in the reference cohort. Low-missingness variables (<=2.5%%: education, cigsPerDay, BPMeds, totChol, BMI, heartRate) should be handled within the same imputation model rather than by case deletion.' % pval)).alignment = WRAP\n ws3.merge_cells(start_row=r+1, start_column=1, end_row=r+6, end_column=3)\n autowidth(ws, [18,20,9,9,11,14,22,13,17,12,30]); autowidth(ws2, [20,130]); autowidth(ws3, [55,90