 = 2σ²(z_{α/2}+z_β)²/δ² per group, δ = 0.8*observed effect, dropout adjust: n/(1-0.15). 90% power → z=1.2816, alpha 0.05 two-sided → 1.96.\n\n8. Conditional power at Week 24 given observed interim data: conditional power = probability of rejecting H0 at end given observed effect. For a completed study (n already enrolled with data), conditional power given current trend: CP = Φ((z_observed - z_{α/2})/1)... Actually for a fully enrolled study where we compute effect at week 24, conditional power under the assumption the observed effect is the true effect: CP = Φ(|z| - z_{1-α/2}) roughly... Standard conditional power at interim with fraction t of information: CP(θ) = Φ((Z_t√t ... Let me use the standard formula: given observed z statistic at interim with information fraction t, conditional power assuming current trend θ = observed: CP = Φ((z_obs - z_{1-α/2}·√(1-... \n\nSimpler approach used commonly: conditional power = Φ((|z_observed| − z_{1−α/2}) / 1) when the study is complete (t=1), which reduces to computing power at observed effect: Φ(|z| − 1.96). Since all Week 24 data here is \"interim at week 24\" relative to the 52-week extension... The task: \"compute the conditional power of the current study at Week 24 given observed interim data.\" Since the primary endpoint is at Week 24 and data is essentially complete, I'll compute conditional power via the standard Lan-Wittes approach: CP = Φ((z_obs·√t − z_{1−α/2}) / √(1−t))? That requires t. \n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: conditional power = power of the observed z-statistic: CP = Φ(|z_observed| − z_{1-α/2}). Given observed data at Week 24, the probability that the final analysis rejects H0, assuming the observed effect is the true effect. With complete data this is just power at the observed effect. I'll use that and document the assumption. Also could do with continuity: treat the observed z, assume future subjects would show the same effect, compute probability final z > 1.96. With the study fully enrolled (all 480), t=1, and the test is already done — conditional power = 1 if p<0.05. To make it meaningful, use observed data completers vs planned N: information fraction t = n_completers/N_planned. Hmm.\n\nI'll compute conditional power in a defensible way: using observed effect and SD, current n, and the formula CP = Φ(|z| − z_{1−α/2}) (power at the observed effect given current sample size), noting that with full Week-24 data this equals the post-hoc power at the observed effect. I'll also present a futility-style calculation assuming observed n and planned N (if observed < planned). Let me check enrollment first — 480 rows, matches plan. So t = n_ITT_with_data/480.\n\nLet me just do: conditional power (current trend) = Φ( (Z_current − z_{1−α/2}·√(1−t)... ). Actually standard formula (Lan & Wittes): CP(θ_a) = Φ( (B(t) + θ_a(1−t) − z_{1−α/2}) / √(1−t) ) where B(t) = Z(t)·√t. With θ_a = observed drift = Z(t)/√t = z·... θ_a in drift units = z/√t. Then CP = Φ( (z√t + (z/√t)(1−t) − z_{α})/√(1−t) ) = Φ( (z/√t − z_α)/√(1−t) ). With t = fraction of planned subjects with week-24 data. I'll compute that.\n\n9. RWE comparison: diabetic_data.csv — A1Ctest result proportions (\"A1Cresult\" tested vs not: column \"A1Cresult\" has values >8, >7, Norm, None) across admission types (decoded via IDS_mapping). Medication utilization: metformin, insulin, glipizide... GLP-1 agents: the dataset has no GLP-1 column (1999-2008; columns include metformin, insulin, and others like \"repaglinide\", \"nateglinide\", \"chlorpropamide\", \"glimepiride\", \"acetohexamide\", \"glipizide\", \"glyburide\", \"tolbutamide\", \"pioglitazone\", \"rosiglitazone\", \"acarbose\", \"miglitol\", \"troglitazone\", \"tolazamide\", \"examide\", \"citoglipton\", \"glyburide-metformin\", etc. \"examide\" might be exenatide analog? Actually examide is a placeholder in this dataset often considered exenatide. GLP-1 agents in this dataset: \"examide\" and \"citoglipton\" are fictional/placeholder. Exenatide was approved 2005. Commonly, \"examide\" is treated as exenatide (GLP-1). I'll report examide as GLP-1 class with note.\n\nTrial baseline medication history: prior_metformin, prior_sulfonylurea, prior_insulin columns. Note protocol says all on stable metformin — check observed prior_metformin proportion (discrepancy!).\n\nCompare proportions: RWE % encounters with metformin used (metformin in {Steady, Up, Down}) vs trial prior_metformin %; insulin similarly; GLP-1: RWE examide vs trial (no prior GLP-1 column — trial excludes? Note as not captured).\n\nOutput workbook with 5 sheets via openpyxl, embed forest plot PNG in \"Subgroup Forest\" sheet.\n\nLet me first explore the trial data quickly, then write one comprehensive script.\n\nLet me look at data specifics.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"id":"msg_d82318c9764243718f5a59f5236a8caf","content":[{"annotations":[],"text":"Dataset downloaded. Now let me explore the trial data structure.","type":"output_text","logprobs":null}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"cd /workspace/task && python3 -c \\\"\\nimport pandas as pd\\ndf = pd.read_csv('DX24_phase3_trial_data.csv')\\nprint(df.shape)\\nprint('sites:', sorted(df.site_id.unique()))\\nprint('arms:', df.treatment_arm.value_counts().to_dict())\\nprint('age range:', df.age.min(), df.age.max())\\nprint('hba1c base range:', df.hba1c_baseline.min(), df.hba1c_baseline.max())\\nprint('disc:', df.discontinued.value_counts().to_dict())\\nprint('itt:', df.itt_population.value_counts().to_dict())\\nprint('pp:', df.pp_population.value_counts().to_dict())\\nprint('week24 nonnull:', df.hba1c_week24.notna().sum())\\nprint('week12 nonnull:', df.hba1c_week12.notna().sum())\\nprint('missing w24 by arm:', df[df.hba1c_week24.isna()].treatment_arm.value_counts().to_dict())\\nprint('prior met:', (df.prior_metformin=='Yes').mean(), 'prior ins:', (df.prior_insulin=='Yes').mean(), 'pri