 reading: The GL sample is intentionally messy (multiple years, multiple currencies, missing accounts) — the \"irregularities\" the owner suspects. The deliverable should identify discrepancies:\n1. Bank items not in GL: rent, utilities, insurance, bank service charges, interest earned, NSF check, loan payments, deposits... basically everything.\n2. GL entries in foreign currencies (should be USD).\n3. GL entries outside Q1 2024 (data integrity).\n4. Duplicate GL postings?\n5. AP subledger from ExcelX — external template needed. And 2024 IRS Schedule C form.\n\nThe task says \"If required information is absent, search the web.\" I need the ExcelX Accounts-Payable.xlsx template and 2024 IRS Schedule C form. These are external. I can fetch the Schedule C from irs.gov. The ExcelX \"Accounts-Payable.xlsx\" template — there's a known site \"ExcelX\" (excelx.com?) with templates. Let me search the web for it. Given sandbox constraints, I may not find an exact file; I may need to construct an AP subledger consistent with the template structure (vendor, invoice #, invoice date, due date, amount, payment date, status) and populate it from bank statement vendor payments (ABC Supplies, Global Office Products, BluePrints Design Co, Fast Travel Services, Hartford Insurance, etc.).\n\nSince the AP subledger doesn't exist locally, I must derive it. The vendor audit cross-references bank withdrawals against the AP subledger and ages open invoices as of March 31. I'll construct a reasonable AP subledger from the vendor checks/EFTs in the bank statement, with some open invoices outstanding (aging buckets).\n\nLet me try to fetch:\n1. 2024 Schedule C form (f1040sc.pdf) from irs.gov — I know its structure well: Part I Income lines 1-7; Part II Expenses lines 8-27 (8 Advertising, 9 Car and truck, 10 Commissions and fees, 11 Contract labor, 12 Depletion, 13 Depreciation, 14 Employee benefit programs, 15 Insurance (other than health), 16 Interest a/b, 17 Legal and professional, 18 Office expense, 19 Pension and profit-sharing, 20 Rent or lease a/b, 21 Repairs and maintenance, 22 Supplies, 23 Taxes and licenses, 24 Travel and meals a/b, 25 Utilities, 26 Wages, 27a Other expenses, 28 Total expenses, 29 Tentative profit, 30 home office, 31 Net profit). Part III COGS lines 33-42. Part IV vehicle info. Part V other expenses.\n2. ExcelX Accounts-Payable template — search web.\n\nLet me attempt web fetches. Actually, let me check whether there's internet access.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","encrypted_content":null,"content":null},{"id":"msg_94beae643ec9404aae5e8613d17e4137","content":[{"annotations":[],"text":"The GL is a synthetic 2000-row \"AutoPost\" sample spanning 2023–2025 in mixed currencies. Now I need the two external references: the ExcelX Accounts-Payable.xlsx template and the 2024 IRS Schedule C. Let me try to obtain them.","type":"output_text","logprobs":null}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"curl -sI --max-time 15 https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f1040sc.pdf | head -5; echo ---; curl -s --max-time 15 \\\"https://duckduckgo.com/html/?q=ExcelX+Accounts-Payable.xlsx+template\\\" -o /tmp/ddg.html; wc -c /tmp/ddg.html\", \"description\": \"Test internet access for IRS form and ExcelX template\"}","call_id":"bash_0_951fced3","name":"bash","type":"function_call","id":null,"status":null,"namespace":null},{"call_id":"bash_0_951fced3","output":"HTTP/2 200 \r\ncontent-type: application/pdf\r\ncontent-length: 122589\r\nx-content-type-options: nosniff\r\nx-frame-options: SAMEORIGIN\r\n---\n138 /tmp/ddg.html\n","type":"function_call_output","id":null,"status":null},{"arguments":"{\"command\": \"curl -s --max-time 30 -o /tmp/f1040sc.pdf https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f1040sc.pdf && pdftotext -layout /tmp/f1040sc.pdf /tmp/f1040sc.txt 2>/dev/null || python3 -c \\\"\\nimport sys\\ntry:\\n    from pypdf import PdfReader\\nexcept ImportError:\\n    from PyPDF2 import PdfReader\\