xpense_Summary.csv** — all 124 Q1 GL expense lines mapped to Schedule C lines (4, 24a, 26) with subtotals ($39,143.94 / $38,334.50 / $40,230.09), 7 bank-derived unrecorded expense mappings, grand total $135,063.53\n\n**Q1_2024_Reconciliation_Exceptions.csv** — 13 exceptions (EXC-001–013) covering accrual/cash timing, missing bank-item entries, the unmatched $1,847.50 check, AP integrity findings, and the severely past-due AP aging.\n\nSources: local files + ExcelX Accounts-Payable.xlsx (downloaded) + IRS 2024 Schedule C (f1040sc--2024.pdf, downloaded).","type":"output_text","logprobs":null}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":null}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"temperature":null,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[],"top_p":null,"background":null,"completed_at":null,"conversation":null,"max_output_tokens":null,"max_tool_calls":null,"moderation":null,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":null,"reasoning":null,"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":null,"status":null,"text":null,"top_logprobs":null,"truncation":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":209388,"input_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2106048},"output_tokens":20210,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":15900},"total_tokens":2351546},"user":null},"reward":0.3409,"failure_reason":null,"opencode_results_fpath":"/Users/cmunley/Documents/Codex/2026-09-02/g/work/Gym/responses_api_agents/opencode_sandboxed_agent/results/faeaf926-2341-45aa-b971-68b11ce7a7ce/export.json","opencode_run_stdout":"Shell: /bin/bash\nInstalled OpenCode\n<think>Let me start by reading the task instructions file to understand what needs to be done.</think>\n<think>Let me read the reference files.</think>\n<think>Now let me read the General_Ledger_Sample.xlsx. It's a binary file; I'll need Python to read it. Let me check what tools are available (python, openpyxl).</think>\n<think>The GL has thousands of random \"AutoPost\" rows spanning 2023-2025. But there should also be specific Q1 2024 entries matching the bank statement. Let me analyze the workbook structure: sheets, header rows, and filter Q1 2024 USD entries. Let me write a proper analysis script.</think>\n<think>The GL is 2000 rows of \"AutoPost\" junk with random dates 2023-2025, random currencies. It's a \"sample\" GL. The task: reconcile Q1 2024. I need to filter Q1 2024 transactions (Jan 1 – Mar 31, 2024). But these GL entries have no cash account entries — they only hit revenue/expense accounts (4000, 4010, 5000, 5010, 6000). No 1000 Cash entries, no rent/utilities/etc. Interesting — the GL sample appears to be synthetic noise, while the bank statement is the \"real\" data.\n\n\nLet me analyze: which accounts appear in GL, and Q1 2024 subsets. Also check for duplicates — the owner \"suspects irregularities\". Discrepancies to find: e.g., NSF check, deposits in transit, outstanding checks, duplicate payments, transposition errors, fees netted from deposits.\n\n\nThe bank statement shows specific transactions. The GL presumably won't match it well since it's random synthetic data. But my job: build the deliverables from what's there. Let me filter GL to Q1 2024 and see what exists per account, check duplicates, and compare totals vs bank statement.\n\n\nAlso required: external \"Accounts-Payable.xlsx subledger template from ExcelX\" and \"2024 IRS Schedule C form\" — I should search the web for these. The AP subledger template from ExcelX — likely from excelx.com templates. Since it can't be provided, I can build an AP subledger based on a template structure. And Schedule C 2024 structure from IRS — I know Schedule C lines well, but let me fetch to confirm.\n\n\nLet me first analyze the GL Q1 2024 data.</think>\n<think>The GL is a \"sample\" with 2000 AutoPost rows spanning 2023–2025, in multiple currencies, and only 5 accounts used (4000, 4010, 5000, 5010, 6000). It doesn't contain the specific bank-statement-like transactions (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.). This GL appears to be synthetic filler data — the task's true \"books\" may need to be derived from the bank statement plus this GL? Hmm.\n\n\nThe task says: reconcile the bank ending balance to the GL cash balance. Bank ending balance: $37,561.95. The GL has no cash (1000) account entries at all. So GL cash balance must be derived. Also the GL only has accounts 4000/4010/5000/5010/6000 — no cash entries, no rent, utilities, insurance, bank charges, loans.\n\n\nSo this is a scenario where the GL \"Sample\" file is the GL (synthetic). The bank statement shows actual Q1 activity. The reconciliation would involve:\n- Bank ending balance 37,561.95\n- GL cash per books: starting 95,000 + deposits recorded - withdrawals recorded. Since GL has no cash entries, the \"book balance\" may be computed from GL revenue/expense entries? That's odd.\n\n\nAlternative reading: The GL sample is intentionally messy (multiple years, multiple currencies, missing accounts) — the \"irregularities\" the owner suspects. The deliverable should identify discrepancies:\n1. Bank items not in GL: rent, utilities, insurance, bank service charges, interest earned, NSF check, loan payments, deposits... basically everything.\n2. GL entries in foreign currencies (should be USD).\n3. GL entries outside Q1 2024 (data integrity).\n4. Duplicate GL postings?\n5. AP subledger from ExcelX — external template needed. And 2024 IRS Schedule C form.\n\n\nThe task says \"If required information is absent, search the web.\" I need the ExcelX Accounts-Payable.xlsx template and 2024 IRS Schedule C form. These are external. I can fetch the Schedule C from irs.gov. The ExcelX \"Accounts-Payable.xlsx\" template — there's a known site \"ExcelX\" (excelx.com?) with templates. Let me search the web for it. Given sandbox constraints, I may not find an exact file; I may need to construct an AP subledger consistent with the template structure (vendor, invoice #, invoice date, due date, amount, payment date, status) and populate it from bank statement vendor payments (ABC Supplies, Global Office Products, BluePrints Design Co, Fast Travel Services, Hartford Insurance, etc.).\n\n\nSince the AP subledger doesn't exist locally, I must derive it. The vendor audit cross-references bank withdrawals against the AP subledger and ages open invoices as of March 31. I'll construct a reasonable AP subledger from the vendor checks/EFTs in the bank statement, with some open invoices outstanding (aging buckets).\n\n\nLet me try to fetch:\n1. 2024 Schedule C form (f1040sc.pdf) from irs.gov — I know its structure well: Part I Income lines 1-7; Part II Expenses lines 8-27 (8 Advertising, 9 Car and truck, 10 Commissions and fees, 11 Contract labor, 12 Depletion, 13 Depreciation, 14 Employee benefit programs, 15 Insurance (other than health), 16 Interest a/b, 17 Legal and professional, 18 Office expense, 19 Pension and profit-sharing, 20 Rent or lease a/b, 21 Repairs and maintenance, 22 Supplies, 23 Taxes and licenses, 24 Travel and meals a/b, 25 Utilities, 26 Wages, 27a Other expenses, 28 Total expenses, 29 Tentative profit, 30 home office, 31 Net profit). Part III COGS lines 33-42. Part IV vehicle info. Part V other expenses.\n2. ExcelX Accounts-Payable template — search web.\n\n\nLet me attempt web fetches. Actually, let me check whether there's internet access.</think>The GL is a synthetic 2000-row \"AutoPost\" sample spanning 2023–2025 in mixed currencies. Now I need the two external references: the ExcelX Accounts-Payable.xlsx template and the 2024 IRS Schedule C. Let me try to obtain them.\n<think>That's the 2025 form (current year since env date is 2026). The task says 2024 Schedule C. Try prior-year form: https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/f1040sc--2024.pdf.</think>\nFound the source — the GL is ExcelX's synthetic dataset and the AP subledger is their Accounts-Payable.xlsx. Downloading it now.\n<think>Now I have a clear picture of both datasets. Key cross-matches between bank withdrawals and AP subledger