sistent reconciliation. \n\n\nNow: is the assumption \"books recorded all checks/ACHs\" consistent with GL? GL doesn't contain rent, utilities, insurance, service charge accounts' postings at all — meaning books did NOT record those expenses! Under accrual accounting, GL expense accounts show 0 for 5020/5030/5040/5050. Hmm. So the \"book cash balance\" derived from the GL alone (no cash entries at all) is undefined; my reconstruction is the pragmatic route, and the accrual GL expense recognition will be handled in the Schedule C mapping + a note that GL expense accounts for rent/utilities/insurance were never posted (exception!). \n\n\nActually wait — this is important for deliverable 2: \"mapping every Q1 expense from the GL to its Schedule C line\". Q1 GL expenses: 5000 COGS 39,143.94, 5010 Travel 38,334.50, 6000 Payroll 40,230.09 (214−124=90 expense rows... actually 39+41+44=124 expense rows, 90 revenue rows). But bank shows cash expenses paid (rent 9600, utilities 1275, insurance 1875, bank charges 105, travel 970.98, payroll 51000, vendor checks 16036.85). The GL expense rows (124 random AutoPost lines in 5 currencies) clearly don't reconcile with cash. The task says \"mapping every Q1 expense from the GL to its Schedule C line\" — so the CSV lists the 124 GL expense rows (each with GL_ID, TxnDate, account, amount, Schedule C line/category, notes) + subtotals per line + grand total. And additionally, my AJEs add expenses (bank charges 105, etc.). Should the CSV include the AJEs? \"every Q1 expense from the GL\" — the GL as adjusted. I'll include the AJE expense lines (AJE refs) too, marked in Notes as adjusting entries. Hmm, but GL_ID for AJE rows — use AJE-01 etc. That works with the GL_ID column.\n\n\nHmm, wait. Should I also add the missing accrual expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, payroll difference) as AJEs? The task says AJEs \"to bring the books into agreement\" — agreement with the bank, i.e., cash-basis book adjustments. The Schedule C draft should